# Before you start

The tutorial is organized as a series of complete examples, from the simplest to the most advanced. Each
example is a folder that can be downloaded, built, and shown in a slide as it is, and each page displays all
its files. The examples can be followed in order, and you can stop once you have what you need.

## The principle

A slide displays interactive 3D in the same way as a browser does: as a web page, inside a frame (`<iframe>`).

<div class="outputs">
<div class="output"><div class="output-name">1. Your program, for the web</div><p>Your C++ OpenGL program, compiled to WebAssembly by Emscripten: <code>project.js</code>, <code>project.wasm</code>.</p></div>
<div class="output"><div class="output-name">2. The page of the demo</div><p><code>slide-frame.html</code>: it runs the program in the frame, and passes it the settings of its address.</p></div>
<div class="output"><div class="output-name">3. The slide</div><p>A frame shows that page; buttons in the slide can drive it, and the arrow keys still go to the next slide.</p></div>
</div>

The slides of the tutorial are made with [reveal.js](https://revealjs.com/) (each example comes with a
slide, `slides.html`), and the same demos are in a talk made with [lhtml-site](https://scholartools.fr/lhtml/).
Both are HTML, where the frame is native.

The small amount of code that connects the program and the slide (two C++ files that read the settings in the
program, and the page `slide-frame.html`) is called the *bridge* in this tutorial.

## What you need

- **A C++ compiler and CMake**, and GLFW for the desktop version of the examples
  (`brew install glfw` on macOS, `sudo apt install libglfw3-dev` on Ubuntu).
- **The Emscripten SDK**, for the web version:

  ```
  git clone https://github.com/emscripten-core/emsdk.git
  cd emsdk
  ./emsdk install latest
  ./emsdk activate latest
  source ./emsdk_env.sh        # in each new terminal, before building for the web
  ```

  (see [Download and install](https://emscripten.org/docs/getting_started/downloads.html)). Emscripten then
  provides `emcmake`, which runs the usual `cmake` command so that it compiles for the web.
- **Python 3**, for a local web server: a browser refuses to load `project.wasm` from a page opened as a file.
- **lhtml-site**, only for the slides made with lhtml-site: `pipx install lhtml-site`
  (see [lhtml-site](https://scholartools.fr/lhtml/)).
- For the examples 3 to 6: the [CGP](https://github.com/drohmer/cgp-2027) library, a C++ library made for
  teaching computer graphics, needed only to recompile them (`git clone https://github.com/drohmer/cgp-2027.git`).
  The two first examples need no library but GLFW.

<div class="callout" id="on-windows"><div class="callout-title">On Windows</div>

The commands of the tutorial are written for macOS and Linux. In PowerShell on Windows:

- `python` (or `py`) instead of `python3`: `python -m http.server 8080`.
- The Emscripten SDK: `.\emsdk install latest` and `.\emsdk activate latest`, then `.\emsdk_env.ps1` in each
  new terminal (`emsdk_env.bat` in cmd). `emcmake` also needs Ninja: `winget install Ninja-build.Ninja`.
- GLFW, for the desktop version of the examples 1 and 2: `vcpkg install glfw3` (see [vcpkg](https://vcpkg.io/)),
  then add `-DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=<vcpkg>\scripts\buildsystems\vcpkg.cmake` to the first `cmake` command. After
  `cmake --build build --config Release`, the program is `build\Release\project.exe`.
- A variable given before a command (`CGP_DIR=../cgp-2027 python3 ...`) is set on its own line:
  `$env:CGP_DIR = "..\cgp-2027"`, then `python scripts\build_examples.py ...`.

The `README.md` at the top of the zip of each example gives these commands for that example. *(These commands
have not been tested on Windows.)*

</div>

## Get the code

Each example page has a zip of the example and of what it needs. Or take everything at once:
[interactive-3d-slides.zip](../../code/interactive-3d-slides.zip) (the bridge, the six examples with their web
builds, the tests). Every file can also be opened alone: [all the files](../../02_more/01_files/index.html).

Each zip unpacks into a folder of its name, with a `README.md` at its top that gives the steps. The web builds
are in the zips (`examples/<example>/web/`): you can show a demo before compiling anything. In the unzipped
folder:

```
python3 -m http.server 8080
```

then open `http://localhost:8080/examples/a1-opengl-cube/slides.html`.

## In reveal.js or in lhtml-site

**reveal.js:** a frame in a `<section>`. `data-src` loads it when its slide comes near, and unloads it after:

```html
<section>
  <iframe class="demo" data-src="web/index.html?speed=0.2"
          style="width: 100%; height: 540px; border: 0"></iframe>
</section>
```

**lhtml-site:** the macro `demo::` writes the frame and sizes it in the layout of the slide (the web build of
the demo is in the folder `src/_demo/` of the presentation):

```
media::
demo::../_demo/index.html?speed=0.2
::
```

The settings of the demo go in its address (`?speed=0.2`): one build serves several slides. The
[example talk in reveal.js](../../decks/reveal.js/index.html) and the
[one in lhtml-site](../../decks/lhtml-site/html/index.html) show the same demos.

<div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-title">The frame takes the keyboard</div>

Once you click in a demo, the keyboard goes to the frame: the arrow keys and the remote control stop
changing the slide. This is how browsers work. The page of the demo (`slide-frame.html`) sends the keys back
to the slides, in both tools.

</div>

